# Prefer hooks for logic

When refactoring React code, **extract logic into a custom hook** (`use-*`) when it is reusable, testable on its own, or makes the component hard to read.

## Move into a hook

- State + setters that form a coherent unit (including `useState` / `useReducer` clusters)
- `useEffect` / subscriptions / timers / event listeners
- Derived values and handlers that depend on that state or props
- Data fetching or mutation orchestration tied to the component lifecycle
- Anything you would copy-paste into a second component

## Keep in the component

- Mostly presentational layout with props passed through
- A single trivial `useState` for local UI (e.g. open/closed) **only if** pulling it out adds no clarity—still prefer a hook if the branch grows or the same pattern appears elsewhere

## Conventions

- Name files `use-*.ts` or `use-*.tsx`; export `useThing` matching the filename.
- Colocate under the feature: e.g. `features/<area>/hooks/use-thing.ts` or next to the component that owns the behavior, consistent with the surrounding folder.
